 "For a steady hum, use the FFT view: a narrow, tall peak shows the exact frequency, such as 50 or 100 Hz for mains hum. The RTA view groups the signal into octave bands and is closer to what you hear. The spectrogram shows the sound over time and reveals noises that only occur now and then, such as a compressor switching on. For low tones choose Z or C weighting, because A weighting strongly suppresses low frequencies.": 1,
 "FFT, RTA and spectrogram measure the same signal but show it differently. FFT has fine frequency resolution and is good for naming a single tone. RTA splits the spectrum into octave bands of logarithmic width, the way the ear perceives pitch, which makes two measurements easier to compare. The spectrogram colours frequency and level over time and is the right tool when the noise comes and goes.": 1,
 "Weighting is the most common mistake. A weighting models the ear's lower sensitivity to low tones, so an annoying hum around 40 to 80 Hz looks much quieter than it feels. To answer \"which frequency is this?\", unweighted Z weighting is the better choice, with C weighting in between.": 1,
 "A built-in phone microphone is designed for speech and becomes less sensitive towards very low frequencies. For tracking down a cause, such as a heat pump, circulation pump or ventilation, that is usually enough, because what matters is where the peak sits, not its exact level. For reliable level figures, an external microphone and calibration help; they do not replace an official assessment of low-frequency noise.": 1,
