 "To cut impulse shopping, put a pause between wanting and paying: convert the price into hours of work at your take-home rate, ask whether it is a need, a want or an impulse, and leave anything non-urgent in the cart for at least 24 hours. After a few weeks of labels you will see which category eats the most work hours.": 1,
 "Impulse buying is rarely a lack of discipline. It comes from moments built for quick decisions: flash-sale countdowns, big sale days such as 11.11 and 12.12, livestreams where someone keeps closing orders, a voucher about to expire, a saved card that needs one tap. In those seconds you compare the price with the original price, not with the money you have to earn, so everything looks like a bargain.": 1,
 "The fix is to change the unit. Divide monthly take-home pay by the hours you actually work to get an hourly rate, then convert the price into hours. A 450,000-dong shirt sounds light, but at 50,000 dong an hour it is nine hours of work, more than a full day. Then label it honestly: need (life is harder without it), want (nice, but can wait) or impulse (wanted it the moment you saw it). Give wants 24 hours and impulses at least three days.": 1,
