 "In Vietnam, parents rarely ask “how hot is it today”. They ask “what time can I take my child to the park, and will we be home before the afternoon rain?”": 1,
 "For young children, check three hourly numbers rather than temperature alone: feels-like temperature, UV index and chance of rain. On hot days the sensible windows are usually before 9am or after 4pm, once UV drops. At UV 6 or higher, use a wide-brimmed hat, thin long sleeves and sunscreen; in rainy season check the hourly rain chance and always carry an umbrella or raincoat.": 1,
 "Children heat up faster than adults and rarely say when they are overheating. In humid air the feels-like temperature can run several degrees above the actual reading, so a 33°C day can feel close to 40°C. That is the number to use when deciding on outdoor play, not the big temperature on the screen.": 1,
 "UV is usually highest from about 10am to 3pm, even under cloud. General guidance is to start protecting skin from UV 3 and to limit midday time outdoors from UV 8. Babies under six months should stay out of direct sun, using clothing and shade rather than sunscreen; ask a paediatrician if unsure.": 1,
 "In many places the rainy season brings afternoon showers: harsh sun in the morning, a quick downpour later. Checking the hourly rain chance lets you go out early and get home before it hits. After rain the air is cooler, but paths are slippery and thunder may linger, so wait for conditions to settle.": 1,
